1)Pull the Alpine image:                docker pull alpine
2)List local images:                    docker images
3)Inspect the image:                    docker inspect alpine
4)Remove the image:                     docker rmi alpine
5)Removing a running container’s imageIf you try to remove an image (docker rmi) while a container (running or stopped) is still using it, Docker will block the request and throw an error.The Error: You’ll see a message like: Error response from daemon: conflict: unable to delete <ID> (must be forced) - image is being used by stopped container <ID>.The Reason: Docker maintains a dependency tree. To ensure system integrity, it prevents you from deleting the underlying "blueprint" (the image) if an instance of it (the container) still exists.The Solution: You must first remove the container (docker rm) before you can remove the image (docker rmi). Alternatively, you can use the -f (force) flag, but this is generally discouraged as it can leave "dangling" layers.

